CAT = "shrmLeadership"; SEG = "LEAD"
# Leadership cluster (19% of the exam): Leadership & Navigation, Ethical Practice, Inclusive Mindset.
Q = [
("A supervisor directs a new hire closely, then delegates more as the hire gains skill. Which theory does this reflect?",
 "Situational leadership", ["Transactional leadership", "Trait theory", "Servant leadership model"],
 "Situational leadership matches the leader's style (directing, coaching, supporting, delegating) to the follower's readiness. Transactional leadership trades rewards for results and does not shift with readiness."),
("A manager inspires staff with a shared vision and pushes them to exceed their own goals. This style is:",
 "Transformational", ["Transactional", "Laissez-faire", "Contingency-based"],
 "Transformational leaders motivate through vision, inspiration and individual development. Transactional leaders rely on exchanges of reward for performance."),
("Which leadership approach puts the growth and well-being of team members ahead of the leader's own status?",
 "Servant leadership", ["Contingency theory", "Great man trait theory", "Transactional leadership"],
 "Servant leadership centers on serving and developing followers. Trait theory looks for inborn leader qualities, and contingency theory matches leaders to situations."),
("Under Herzberg's two-factor theory, which of these is a hygiene factor?",
 "Working conditions", ["Recognition for good work", "A sense of achievement", "Growth opportunities"],
 "Hygiene factors such as working conditions, pay and policy prevent dissatisfaction but do not motivate. Recognition, achievement and growth are motivators."),
("Vroom's expectancy theory says motivation depends on effort leading to performance, performance leading to reward, and:",
 "Valuing the outcome", ["Fair comparison with peers", "Meeting basic needs first", "Setting specific goals"],
 "Expectancy theory multiplies expectancy, instrumentality and valence, the value a person places on the reward. Fair comparison with peers is equity theory, and specific goals are goal-setting theory."),
("An employee cuts her effort after learning a peer earns more for the same work. Which theory explains this?",
 "Equity theory", ["Goal-setting theory", "Expectancy theory", "Two-factor theory"],
 "Equity theory holds that people compare their input-to-outcome ratio with others and act to restore balance when they see unfairness, for example by reducing effort."),
("An HR manager wins approval for a new program by showing executives cost data and pilot results. Which influence tactic is this?",
 "Rational persuasion", ["Personal appeal to loyalty", "Building a coalition", "Leading by personal example"],
 "Rational persuasion uses logic, data and evidence. Personal appeal draws on friendship or loyalty, and coalition building gathers allies before asking."),
("A director wants HR to launch a wellness app next week. Legal has not reviewed the vendor contract. What should the HR specialist do?",
 "Flag the missing review", ["Launch now, review later", "Delay it a full year", "Let the vendor set terms"],
 "Navigating the organization means meeting the leader's goal without skipping the controls that protect it. Flagging the missing review lets the director decide with the risk in view; a year's delay is not needed to fix it."),
("An HR generalist is asked to lead a project with no defined goals or deadline. What is the best first step?",
 "Agree scope with the sponsor", ["Build a detailed Gantt chart", "Recruit a large project team", "Survey all employees"],
 "Managing HR initiatives starts with a clear scope, goals and timeline agreed with the sponsor. A schedule or team built before that has nothing to plan against."),
("Supervisors are quietly not using a new scheduling policy. What should HR do first?",
 "Learn what is stopping them", ["Report them to leadership", "Resend the policy by email", "Withdraw the policy"],
 "Resistance is best handled by learning its causes, such as unclear steps or a real flaw in the policy. Escalating or repeating the message without knowing why rarely changes behavior."),
("An HR project is two weeks behind because a vendor missed a deadline. What should the project lead do?",
 "Alert the sponsor with a new plan", ["Wait for the vendor to catch up", "Quietly cut the testing phase", "Blame the vendor to all staff"],
 "Good project leadership reports slippage promptly with a recovery plan. Quietly cutting testing hides a risk, and waiting gives the sponsor no chance to adjust."),
("A manager asks HR to skip the background-check step to hire a contractor faster. The step is required by policy. Best response?",
 "Offer a faster compliant route", ["Approve the exception once", "Refuse with no reason given", "Let the manager decide alone"],
 "HR supports business speed while keeping required controls, for example by expediting the check. Refusing without explanation damages the relationship, and a one-off exception sets a precedent."),
("Which situation is a conflict of interest for an HR recruiter?",
 "Screening a relative's application", ["Posting one job on two job boards at once", "Sourcing from a rival's laid-off staff", "Interviewing on a weekend"],
 "A conflict of interest arises when a personal relationship could bias a work decision. The recruiter should disclose it and step out of that hiring decision."),
("A senior manager asks the HR specialist why a coworker is on medical leave. What should she do?",
 "Keep the reason private", ["Share it because he is senior", "Share only the general diagnosis", "Share it if he promises discretion"],
 "Medical information is confidential and shared only with those who need it to do their jobs, and curiosity is not a need. Seniority does not change that."),
("An employee tells HR about a supervisor harassing a peer and asks HR to keep it secret. What should HR do?",
 "Explain HR must act on it", ["Promise to keep it fully secret", "Wait for the peer to complain", "Tell the supervisor right away"],
 "HR keeps reports as confidential as possible but cannot promise secrecy once it knows of possible harassment, because the employer must act. Alerting the supervisor first could invite retaliation."),
("A vendor bidding on the benefits contract offers the HR manager free conference tickets. Best response?",
 "Decline while the bid is open", ["Accept and tell no one", "Accept and attend on personal time", "Accept and score the bid lower"],
 "Accepting gifts from a bidder during a selection creates at least the appearance of a conflict of interest. Lowering the vendor's score to compensate would be its own bias."),
("A privacy policy enrolls no one in a data program unless they choose to join. This is:",
 "Opt-in consent", ["Opt-out consent", "Anonymity", "Data minimization"],
 "Opt-in means nothing happens until the person agrees. Opt-out enrolls people by default and lets them leave."),
("Survey answers that HR can link to individuals but promises not to share are:",
 "Confidential", ["Anonymous", "De-identified", "Aggregated"],
 "Confidential responses can be traced to the respondent but are protected. Anonymous responses cannot be traced to anyone at all, so HR must not promise anonymity when it collects names."),
("HR finds that its own payroll error underpaid 12 workers last month. What should HR do?",
 "Disclose it and correct the pay", ["Fix it quietly next quarter", "Correct it only if someone asks", "Spread repayment over a year"],
 "Professional integrity means owning mistakes and fixing them promptly. Delaying or hiding the correction leaves workers short and risks wage claims."),
("During an investigation, a witness asks HR whether the accused will be fired. What should HR say?",
 "It depends on the findings", ["Yes, he will be fired", "No, nothing will happen", "The outcome is decided"],
 "An impartial investigation reaches no conclusion before the facts are in. Predicting an outcome to a witness suggests bias and can taint the process."),
("A manager asks HR to backdate a written warning before a termination. What should HR do?",
 "Refuse and record the request", ["Backdate it with a short note", "Backdate it if legal agrees", "Ask payroll to handle it"],
 "Backdating creates a false record, which is dishonest and damaging if the termination is challenged. HR should refuse and document the request."),
("An ethics hotline lets people raise misconduct concerns without giving their names. This protects:",
 "Reporter anonymity", ["Accuracy of the data", "Record retention schedules", "Investigator neutrality"],
 "Anonymous reporting channels lower the fear of retaliation, so more misconduct comes to light. They do not by themselves ensure accurate data or due process."),
("An HR assistant processing raises is asked by a friend what her peers will earn. What should the assistant do?",
 "Decline to share others' pay", ["Share only the pay range", "Share it if the friend stays quiet", "Refer the friend to payroll for it"],
 "HR staff who see pay data in their role must keep it confidential. Employees may discuss their own pay, but HR access is not for sharing others' figures."),
("An HR specialist finds an error in a turnover report already sent to leaders. Best action?",
 "Send a correction promptly", ["Wait to see if anyone notices", "Fix it in next month's report", "Note the data source was wrong"],
 "Leaders may act on the wrong figure, so a prompt correction is the honest and useful step. Waiting a month lets bad decisions stand."),
("A manager rates a candidate highly mainly because they went to the same college. This bias is:",
 "Ingroup bias", ["Halo effect", "Recency bias", "Central tendency"],
 "Ingroup bias favors people seen as part of one's own group. The halo effect lets one strong trait color every rating, whatever the group."),
("Small everyday slights that signal to someone they do not belong are called:",
 "Microaggressions", ["Cultural taxation", "Identity covering", "Stereotype threat"],
 "Microaggressions are brief, often unintended remarks or acts that demean a group. Cultural taxation is extra unrecognized work put on underrepresented staff."),
("Extra unpaid diversity work expected of underrepresented employees is known as:",
 "Cultural taxation", ["Microaggression", "Identity covering", "Imposter syndrome"],
 "Cultural taxation describes the added, often unrewarded duties, such as serving on every diversity panel, placed on employees from underrepresented groups."),
("An employee downplays her accent, faith and family life to fit in at work. This is called:",
 "Identity covering", ["Imposter syndrome", "Groupthink", "Social loafing"],
 "Covering means toning down a known identity to blend in. Imposter syndrome is doubting one's own competence despite evidence of success."),
("In Hofstede's model, a culture that accepts an unequal spread of authority scores high on:",
 "Power distance", ["Uncertainty avoidance", "Individualism", "Long-term orientation"],
 "Power distance measures how far less powerful members accept unequal power. Uncertainty avoidance measures comfort with ambiguity."),
("An employee asks to trade shifts to observe a religious holiday. A coworker has agreed to cover. What should HR do?",
 "Approve the swap", ["Deny it to avoid precedent", "Ask for a letter from clergy", "Require unpaid leave instead"],
 "Title VII requires religious accommodation unless it would cause undue hardship, and a voluntary swap costs the employer almost nothing. Asking for clergy proof for a simple request is not needed."),
("A veterans' employee resource group asks to bar nonveterans from its events. What should HR advise?",
 "Keep events open to all", ["Approve the restriction", "Disband the group", "Hold events off-site only"],
 "Inclusive ERGs center a shared identity but stay open to allies, which avoids exclusion claims and builds understanding. Disbanding the group overcorrects."),
("Interview panels keep choosing finalists from a single university. What should HR do first?",
 "Review the selection data", ["Ban hires from that university", "Replace every panel member", "Set a hiring quota"],
 "Data on applicants, scores and outcomes shows whether sourcing or scoring is the cause. Quotas based on protected traits are unlawful, and bans punish candidates."),
("A team member is repeatedly talked over in meetings. Her manager asks HR for advice. Best advice?",
 "Set and enforce speaking norms", ["Tell her to speak louder and sooner", "Move her to another team for now", "Send the whole team to a workshop"],
 "Clear meeting norms enforced by the leader fix the behavior where it happens. Moving her penalizes the person being talked over."),
("A pay audit finds women in one role earn 8% less with no job-related reason. What should HR recommend?",
 "Adjust the affected salaries", ["Run unconscious bias training", "Recheck at next year's audit", "Keep the results confidential"],
 "Training alone leaves the gap in place. An unexplained gap in pay for equal work should be corrected, and the Equal Pay Act bars lowering anyone's pay to close it."),
("Which measure best shows whether diversity extends from entry roles to senior ranks?",
 "Representation by job level", ["Total headcount by year", "Average tenure by team", "Training hours per employee"],
 "Comparing representation at each level shows whether people advance, not just whether they are hired. Headcount and tenure do not show distribution."),
("A US manager will lead a team in a culture where public criticism causes loss of face. Best approach to feedback?",
 "Give it one-on-one", ["Give it in team meetings", "Skip critical feedback", "Send it only through HR"],
 "Private feedback respects the norm while still addressing performance. Avoiding feedback leaves problems unsolved."),
]
